  "minimum_safe_summary": "This book review discusses Ralph M. Evans's 'The Perception of Color,' which proposes a five-dimensional system for color description, adding 'lightness' and 'brilliance' to the classic three. Evans argues that colorimetry is insufficient for color perception and that stimuli equal in luminance are not equally bright, necessitating the 'brilliance' variable. The reviewer critiques Evans's views, suggesting methodological differences explain brightness-luminance discrepancies and noting inconsistencies in Evans's claims about achromatic components in the spectrum. Limitations of the book include a lack of a unified glossary and sparse illustrations, as well as the reviewer's difficulty in separating Evans's personal opinions from broader scientific interpretations.",
